  "body": "I'm still investigating exactly what is going on here, but between https://github.com/WICG/interventions/issues/16 and https://chromestatus.com/feature/5851021045661696 there is some indication that Chrome shipped a restriction on how much iframes can navigate the top-level frame, beyond the currently-specified restrictions based on sandboxing.\r\n\r\nI have found some evidence that:\r\n\r\n- This is based on [sticky activation, not transient activation](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/blink-dev/c/Xi8-y4ySjA4/m/9_96y_F6CAAJ)\r\n- Something changed in Chrome 102 which is causing people to notice this now; possibly [something about resetting sticky activation on redirects](https://github.com/WICG/interventions/issues/16#issuecomment-1147627747)\r\n\r\nSupposedly Chrome shipped this in Chrome 68, but every few releases someone shows up in https://github.com/WICG/interventions/issues/16 complaining that something new is broken, so it's not clear exactly what Chrome is doing here.\r\n\r\nWe should:\r\n\r\n- Figure out exactly what Chrome has implemented\r\n- See if other browsers are interested in doing so as well\r\n- If so, spec it and write WPTs.\r\n\r\n/cc @natechapin @shivanigithub",
